===Etymology===
From {{inh|lv|ine-bsl-pro|*śwṓ}}, {{m|ine-bsl-pro|*śunas}}, from {{inh|lv|ine-pro|*ḱwṓ}}, genitive {{m|ine-pro|*ḱunós}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ḱunés}}. An older Latvian form {{m|lv|*so}} is now found only in one dialect (Nīgrande) as a word to call dogs. Cognates include {{cog|lt|šuõ}} (genitive {{m|lt|šuñs}}, dialectal {{m|lt|šunis}}), {{cog|prg|sunnis|sunis}}, {{cog|gem-pro|*hundaz}} ({{cog|got|𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃}}, {{cog|non|hundr}}, {{cog|is|hundur}}, {{cog|sv|hund}}, {{cog|goh|hunt}}, {{cog|ang|hund}}, {{cog|de|Hund}}, {{cog|nl|hond}}, {{cog|en|hound}}), {{cog|ga|cú}}, {{cog|cy|ci}}, plural {{m|cy|cŵn}}, {{cog|sa|श्वन्|tr=śván}}, genitive {{m|sa|शुनः|शुनस्}}, {{cog|ae|𐬯𐬞𐬁}}, genitive plural {{m|ae|𐬯𐬎𐬥𐬀𐬨}}, {{cog|xcl|շուն}}, {{cog|grc|κύων}}, {{cog|la|canis}} ({{cog|it|cane}}, {{cog|pt|cão}}, {{cog|fr|chien}}, {{cog|es|can}}).<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

